<h2 id="the-first-year">What the first year looks like</h2>

<p>Recovery has its own article, <a href="/blog/beard-transplant-recovery-timeline">the beard transplant recovery timeline</a>, so only the outline belongs here. Small crusts form around each graft and sit on the face for roughly the first week, with redness and some swelling. The transplanted hairs commonly shed somewhere between the second and fourth weeks while the follicles stay in the skin. New growth usually begins around three to four months, unevenly and finer than it will end up, and the result is fairly judged at around ten to twelve months.</p>

<p>Two practical points are specific to the face. It cannot be hidden under a cap, so most people plan the first week or two around anything client-facing. And beard skin is more prone than the scalp to inflamed follicles and ingrown hairs as new hairs break through; DermNet notes that in the beard area irritant <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/folliculitis" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">folliculitis</a> is known as pseudofolliculitis barbae. It is usually minor and treatable, but it should be looked at rather than squeezed.</p>

<p>When trimming and razor shaving are allowed is decided by your doctor at a review, according to how the grafts have healed, not by a date printed on a website. Trimming is usually cleared first.</p>

<h2 id="what-it-cannot-do">What a beard transplant cannot do</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>It cannot create more hair than your donor area holds.</strong> A full, dense beard for someone with almost no native growth may not be affordable in grafts, or may need to be staged across sessions.</li>
  <li><strong>It cannot turn scalp hair into beard hair.</strong> Texture and growth length stay those of the donor site.</li>
  <li><strong>It cannot promise a density or a survival figure.</strong> Not every graft survives, native hair around the grafts can shed temporarily, and some zones need a planned second session.</li>
  <li><strong>It cannot quickly fix a poor angle.</strong> Grafts set to bristle keep bristling, and correcting them means further surgery.</li>
  <li><strong>It does not stop change.</strong> The native beard around the grafts still ages, greys and shifts over the decades.</li>
</ul>

<p>Photographs are the other place expectations get set. A beard photographed freshly trimmed, oiled and lit from the side can look very different under overhead office lighting, and <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">reading before-and-after photos critically</a> applies to faces just as much as scalps.</p>

<p>If you are weighing it up, bring the question you actually have. It is not whether beard transplants work in general, but whether your face, your donor area and your scalp's likely future make it a good trade for you. That is what an examination by a registered medical practitioner is for, and it is a question an honest consultation will sometimes answer with not yet.</p>

